{% load static %}
{% load nilpoint_tags %}
{% partialdef item_card %}
    {% comment %}
One item tile, drawn by both the location and the inventory panel so the
two cannot drift apart.

    `card_item` is the LocationItem or the InventoryItem. The card
    reads the record rather than its class: `is_in_inventory` picks
    wording (Drop or Take) and the item's own `can_drop` / `can_take`
    decides whether it is offered at all. Inspect is always first in
    the footer and always the same `item_detail` action
    {% endcomment %}
    <li class="nilpoint-item-card">
        <div class="nilpoint-item-card-media">
            <img class="nilpoint-item-card-graphic"
                 src="{% static card_item.item.graphic_safe %}"
                 alt=""
                 width="64"
                 height="64"
                 loading="lazy">
            {% comment %}
		 A bar, not a heading: the panel's h3 is the only
		 heading, and the name is the card's own visible text next to its
		 buttons. More like li elements than headings. Seems best.
            {% endcomment %}
            <p class="nilpoint-item-card-title">{{ card_item.item.name }}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="nilpoint-item-card-footer">
            {% if card_item.is_in_inventory %}
                {% nilpoint_action_url game 'item_detail' inventory_item=card_item.id as inspect_url %}
            {% else %}
                {% nilpoint_action_url game 'item_detail' location_item=card_item.id as inspect_url %}
            {% endif %}
            <button type="button"
                    class="nilpoint-item-card-action nilpoint-item-card-action--inspect"
                    hx-get="{{ inspect_url }}"
                    hx-target="#nilpoint-item-detail-panel"
                    hx-swap="innerHTML">
                <span class="nilpoint-item-card-glyph" aria-hidden="true">🔍</span>
                <span class="nilpoint-item-card-label">Inspect</span>
            </button>
            {% if card_item.is_in_inventory %}
                {% if card_item.item.can_drop %}
                    {% include "nilpoint/item_card.jinja2#item_move_action" with move_action="drop_item" move_key="inventory_item" move_id=card_item.id move_label="Drop" move_glyph="⬇" %}
                {% endif %}
            {% else %}
                {% if card_item.item.can_take %}
                    {% include "nilpoint/item_card.jinja2#item_move_action" with move_action="take_item" move_key="location_item" move_id=card_item.id move_label="Take" move_glyph="✋" %}
                {% endif %}
            {% endif %}
        </div>
    </li>
{% endpartialdef %}
{% partialdef item_move_action %}
    {% comment %}
    The card's right-hand action: Take in the world, Drop in the inventory.
    `move_vals` is the JSON htmx sends with the POST: the record's id under the
    name its handler reads (`location_item` / `inventory_item`). The panel
    reloads itself off the change triggers rather than off this response, hence
    swap="none".

    `hx-vals` is written by hand, so two template traps apply. The attribute is
    single-quoted because JSON needs double quotes around its keys, and a
    single-quoted attribute carries them without escaping. And a space sits
    between the value and the closing brace: a `{{ }}` tag flush against a
    literal `}` reads as `}}}` to Django's lexer and renders an extra brace,
    which is invalid JSON that htmx drops in silence.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% nilpoint_action_url game move_action as move_url %}
    <button type="button"
            class="nilpoint-item-card-action nilpoint-item-card-action--move"
            hx-post="{{ move_url }}"
            hx-vals='{"{{ move_key }}": {{ move_id }} }'
            hx-swap="none">
        <span class="nilpoint-item-card-glyph" aria-hidden="true">{{ move_glyph }}</span>
        <span class="nilpoint-item-card-label">{{ move_label }}</span>
    </button>
{% endpartialdef %}
